WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.949
My name is Andrea Sozzi Sabatini,

00:00:06.949 --> 00:00:09.301
and I'm the CEO and founder of Etrurio.

00:00:09.301 --> 00:00:12.800
It's a farm where it aims to produce

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.928
top-quality olive oil in Tuscany.

00:00:14.928 --> 00:00:16.993
Today, I'm going to walk you through

00:00:16.993 --> 00:00:20.301
how Marco restores this beautiful

00:00:20.301 --> 00:00:23.291
583-year-old fresco.

00:00:27.462 --> 00:00:29.278
The first work that we have to do

00:00:29.278 --> 00:00:31.570
is to consolidate the walls.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:35.660
The first thing is
really to take the stones

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.050
that are unstable, take them off,

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:40.560
consolidate whatever is underneath,

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:45.113
and then put the stones
back to their places.

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:50.740
In this large fresco, there
are cracks here and there,

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:54.970
so the next step is to
consolidate these cracks.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:57.600
The cracks get consolidated

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.983
with resin, special resin.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:04.730
The next step is now
to remove the plasters

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:07.290
that had been painted over the fresco

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.048
in the last few centuries.

00:01:09.048 --> 00:01:11.136
And to do that, Marco, the restorer,

00:01:11.136 --> 00:01:13.912
needs to get archeological scalpel

00:01:13.912 --> 00:01:16.760
and remove every single bit and pieces

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.810
out of this huge surface.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:23.360
And it's very delicate work,

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.570
because if you are not precise enough

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.636
of course you can damage
the fresco itself.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.410
Now that the plaster has been removed,

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:40.850
we can apply Japanese paper

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:45.280
soaked with water and ammonium carbonate.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.530
This is a very delicate process.

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:50.512
And the reason why we
are doing it is because,

00:01:50.512 --> 00:01:52.520
thanks to the Japanese paper,

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:56.560
all the dirt and all
the external component

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.060
get away from the fresco.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.370
And it's a process that
can last up to six months.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.260
After all this work, as you can see,

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:05.960
the fresco is revealing itself.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:08.760
Some of the colors are now vivid.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.180
What was underneath the plaster

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:15.280
fortunately is still there for most of it.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.130
We are actually applying
again Japanese paper

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:21.890
soaked with a different
product, carbonate hydroxide.

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:25.960
And this is a very important
part of the fresco restoration,

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:29.349
because it stabilize the fresco itself.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.510
Now that all these
processes are completed,

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:43.870
Marco can start the second
phase of the restoration.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:44.703
As you can see,

00:02:44.703 --> 00:02:46.770
there's a lot of gaps in the fresco,

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.140
because some of the paint has faded,

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.520
some others were lost.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:54.680
He's going to use a
very tiny, small brush,

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.850
and he is going to use a natural pigment,

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:01.230
like they were used back in the 1400s.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:02.390
There has been a surveillance

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:05.890
of the Italian authority over this fresco.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:07.180
We are not allowed in Italy

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:10.563
to paint back anything
that is not present.

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:20.050
The whole restoration took a long time.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:21.650
It took 18 months.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:23.560
We are very proud that

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:27.227
we didn't lose this
piece of art in Tuscany.

